============================================================
PART 3 — SERVER CONSOLIDATION
============================================================

OBJECTIVE:
Reduce the number of VMs while continuing to provide two web services.

BEFORE CONSOLIDATION — 3 VMs:
- WebServer1
- WebServer2
- LoadBalancer

AFTER CONSOLIDATION — 2 VMs:
- One VM hosts both web services on separate ports.
- One VM hosts Nginx as the load balancer.

STEP 6: HOST TWO WEB SERVICES ON ONE VM

On WebServer1, stop its existing Python web server by pressing Ctrl+C.
Run:

    mkdir -p ~/web1
    mkdir -p ~/web2
    echo "<h1>Response from Consolidated Web Server 1</h1>" > ~/web1/index.html
    echo "<h1>Response from Consolidated Web Server 2</h1>" > ~/web2/index.html

Open Terminal 1 on the consolidated web VM:
    cd ~/web1
    python3 -m http.server 8081 --bind 0.0.0.0

Open a second terminal on the same VM:
    cd ~/web2
    python3 -m http.server 8082 --bind 0.0.0.0

Keep both terminals open. The services now use ports 8081 and 8082.

STEP 7: UPDATE NGINX FOR THE CONSOLIDATED SERVICES

On LoadBalancer, open:
    sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/default

Replace the contents with this configuration:
    upstream web_servers {
        server CONSOLIDATED_WEB_VM_IP:8081;
        server CONSOLIDATED_WEB_VM_IP:8082;
    }

    server {
        listen 80;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://web_servers;
        }
    }

Replace CONSOLIDATED_WEB_VM_IP with the actual IP address of WebServer1. Save with Ctrl+O, Enter, then Ctrl+X.

Test and reload:
    sudo nginx -t
    sudo systemctl reload nginx

Test:
    curl http://localhost
    curl http://localhost

============================================================
PART 4 — FAULT TOLERANCE
============================================================

STEP 8: CONFIGURE NGINX TO HANDLE AN UNAVAILABLE BACKEND

On LoadBalancer, open:
    sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/default

For the original two-server setup on port 8080, use:
    upstream web_servers {
        server 192.168.100.11:8080 max_fails=2 fail_timeout=10s;
        server 192.168.100.12:8080 max_fails=2 fail_timeout=10s;
    }

    server {
        listen 80;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://web_servers;
        }
    }

Replace the example IP addresses with the actual WebServer1 and WebServer2 IP addresses. Save with Ctrl+O, Enter, then Ctrl+X.

Test and reload:
    sudo nginx -t
    sudo systemctl reload nginx

NOTE:
This configuration applies to the original two-server setup on port 8080, not the consolidated setup on ports 8081 and 8082. Nginx uses passive failure detection; a backend may not be marked unavailable until requests fail.

============================================================
STEP 9: SIMULATE A WEB SERVER FAILURE
============================================================

BEFORE FAILURE:
Make sure both web servers and LoadBalancer are running. On LoadBalancer, run:

    curl http://localhost
    curl http://localhost
    curl http://localhost
    curl http://localhost

Expected: Responses from both backend servers.

SIMULATE FAILURE:
1. Go to the WebServer1 terminal where the Python server is running.
2. Press Ctrl+C to stop WebServer1.
3. Keep WebServer2 and LoadBalancer running.

TEST AFTER FAILURE:
On LoadBalancer, run:

    curl http://localhost
    curl http://localhost
    curl http://localhost

EXPECTED RESULT:
After Nginx detects WebServer1's failure, requests should be served by WebServer2.

RESTORE WEB SERVER 1:
On WebServer1, run:

    cd ~/web
    python3 -m http.server 8080 --bind 0.0.0.0

Then test from LoadBalancer:
    curl http://localhost
    curl http://localhost

NOTE:
The max_fails and fail_timeout settings provide basic passive failure handling. They do not guarantee instant failure detection or eliminate every possible interruption.

